Deploy software automatically to domain-joined Windows client computers using Group Policy Software Installation.

⸻

Lab Environment
	•	Windows Server 2022 (Domain Controller)
	•	Windows 10 Client
	•	Active Directory Domain Services
	•	Group Policy Management
	•	VMware Workstation
	•	Shared Folder (\ServerName\Software)

⸻

Software Used
	•	7-Zip (MSI)

⸻

Steps Performed

1. Prepared the Software Repository

	•	Created C:\Software on the domain controller.
	•	Shared the folder with appropriate permissions.
	•	Copied the 7-Zip MSI installer into the shared folder.

⸻

2. Created a New Group Policy Object

Created a new GPO named:

Deploy 7-Zip

⸻

3. Configured Software Installation

Navigated to:

Computer Configuration

→ Policies

→ Software Settings

→ Software Installation

Selected:

New → Package

Used the UNC path instead of the local C: drive.

Example:

\SERVERNAME\Software\7z.msi

Deployment Method:

Assigned

4. Linked the GPO

Initially linked the GPO to the HR OU.

The deployment did not occur because the target computer was located inside the Workstations OU.

Moved the GPO link to the Workstations OU.

5. Updated Group Policy

Executed:

gpupdate /force

Restarted the Windows client.

6. Verified Deployment

After rebooting:
	•	7-Zip installed automatically.
	•	The software appeared under Programs and Features.
	•	Users could launch the application without manually installing it.

Troubleshooting

Issue 1

Software did not install.

Cause:

The GPO was linked to the wrong Organizational Unit.

Resolution:

Linked the GPO to the Workstations OU where the computer account resided.

⸻

Issue 2

Attempted to deploy Notepad++ but accidentally downloaded an Internet Shortcut (.url) instead of the MSI installer.

Observed:
	•	File Type: Internet Shortcut (.url)
	•	File Size: 225 bytes

Expected:
	•	Windows Installer Package (.msi)
	•	File size of several megabytes

Resolution:

Verified the download source and learned to distinguish between a web shortcut and a genuine MSI installer.

